WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.260
Did the official Star Wars website accidentally
spoil a plot point in The Rise of Skywalker?

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:07.600
It sure looks that way.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.600
In the newest available footage from the film,
first released at Disney's D23 Expo on August

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:19.550
24 and then released online August 26, Rey
is briefly seen cloaked in a black robe with

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:23.600
a stern look on her face as she brandishes
a double-bladed red lightsaber.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:29.039
Her pale skin, rigid movements, attire, and
new weapon of choice that calls to mind the

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:34.440
saber that Darth Maul wielded all fueled what
has become a popular fan theory about "Dark

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.080
Rey": she's a vision of a possible future.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.640
Fans thought they would only be able to confirm
or cancel out that theory by watching The

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.320
Rise of Skywalker in theaters, but on August
27, eagle-eyed Star Wars enthusiasts spotted

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.300
a slip-up published on the franchise's website.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:56.860
Just after Lucasfilm took the stage at D23
to talk all things Rise of Skywalker, StarWars.com

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:00.772
ran a news piece breaking down what was shown
at the presentation.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:05.580
The final paragraph focused on the new Rise
of Skywalker footage, highlighting Dark Rey.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.360
One Redditor grabbed a screenshot of the first
version of the post on StarWars.com, which

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:11.140
read in part,

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.500
"The wise words were also part of the first
trailer for the film, but the new footage,

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:21.079
including a moment where Rey appears to toss
a fully-ignited lightsaber, and of course

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:25.300
the vision of Rey with a reticulated red blade,
was nothing short of thrilling."

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.750
The article has since been updated to replace
the word "vision" with "sight."

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:33.600
On one hand, it could be taken as an accidental
leak.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.829
Maybe the StarWars.com staff had some inside
information, and that top-secret intel slipped

00:01:38.829 --> 00:01:42.120
through the cracks and made its way into the
published version of the article.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:46.920
If that's the case, it likely wasn't intentional,
but the slip-up is pretty big.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:48.600
"This was a mistake!"

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.500
On the other hand, this could just be an unfortunate
phrasing.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:57.789
Star Wars secrets are usually kept more secure
than John Wick's stash of weapons, so it seems

00:01:57.789 --> 00:02:03.119
pretty unlikely that the StarWars.com editorial
team would be privy to plot details that the

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:04.300
general public isn't.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:09.560
As many Redditors have noted, the writer was
possibly thinking more of the definition of

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:13.100
"sight" when writing the post, but used the
synonym "vision."

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.310
It's plausible that the writer was merely
trying to speak about the appearance of Dark

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:20.100
Rey, rather than imply something plot-related.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:26.459
The initial use of "vision" in the StarWars.com
article implies that Dark Rey is a premonition

00:02:26.459 --> 00:02:31.730
of a possible dark future, a pretty common
thing in Star Wars, and the subsequent removal

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:35.520
of the word could be interpreted as the website
sealing off a leak.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.920
Or it could have been an honest mistake that
led to a semantics argument.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:41.280
“My Vision.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.980
They really did take everything from me."

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:45.240
"You set the terms.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:46.240
You can change them."

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:51.060
Though "vision" is just one word, there's
far too much room for interpretation when

00:02:51.079 --> 00:02:55.959
it's used in any other way besides talking
about a Force-fueled hallucination, especially

00:02:55.959 --> 00:03:00.769
since the hottest theory regarding Dark Rey
is that she is in fact some kind of antagonistic

00:03:00.769 --> 00:03:03.660
apparition that appears at a pivotal moment
in the film.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:10.000
Fans may be stuck on the idea that Dark Rey
is definitely a vision in The Rise of Skywalker,

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.000
appearing when Rey is questioning her alignment
with the Force or when Kylo Ren is imagining

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:19.140
what would happen if Rey turned to the dark
side and joined the First Order, but there

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.340
are plenty more possible explanations.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.260
Dark Rey could be a clone, or she might be
Rey's evil twin sister who waited until the

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:27.520
eleventh hour to show up.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.100
Or she could be real.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:34.460
Rey could fall under the influence of Emperor
Palpatine, whom actress Daisy Ridley has said

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:34.960
is

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:38.520
"[...] very, you know, instrumental to the
plot of the film."

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:41.140
Will Dark Rey be revealed as a vision?

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.880
Is she the new emissary for Palpatine, or
this generation's answer to Darth Maul?

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:47.080
Is she a clone?

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:52.269
Or is this all very clever, intentional misdirection
from Lucasfilm, with the semantic slip-up

00:03:52.269 --> 00:03:56.800
published in the StarWars.com article actually
meant to make us even more confused?

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:02.380
We'll only know for sure when Star Wars: The
Rise of Skywalker hits theaters December 20.

